// Classic vaporwave README banner for ULTRA-SATISFACTORY.
//
//   node examples/ultra-satisfactory/src/26-vaporwave_opus_5.5.mjs
//
// Regenerates ../assets/26-vaporwave_opus_5.5.svg. Plain Node, no deps,
// deterministic (seeded PRNG, no clock). The .md beside the assets is
// hand-written, not generated by this script.
//
// The look is 2011-era vaporwave collage in the manner of the Floral Shoppe
// cover (catalogue entry vap-01): a flat hot-pink ground, a black-and-pink
// checker floor in one-point perspective in the lower right, a marble bust on
// a white block at the left, a mint two-line title with Japanese at the top
// right, and mid-90s window chrome used as picture frames. Nothing is traced
// from that cover or from any font: the bust is an original profile drawing
// (the famous one is a frontal Helios head), the Latin capitals and the
// kana/kanji are monoline strokes defined below, the window type is a 5x7
// bitmap font, and the emblem is this app's hexagon-and-cog, not the cover's
// warped grid. No <text>, no filters, no external anything.
//
// Japanese used (real, and meant):
//   ウルトラ・サティスファクトリー   "Ultra Satisfactory" in katakana
//   工場の友                         "the factory's friend" (a companion)
//
// Loop maths
//   Floor: a row of tiles at depth u projects to y = HZ + NEAR*Z0/u, so one
//   @keyframes ("fl") samples that hyperbola for a band's whole life, from the
//   far edge to well past the viewer (LIFE rows, M samples per row). Every
//   black band runs it with a negative delay of a whole number of rows, so all
//   band edges share the same sample points and meet exactly. Bands are two
//   rows apart; one set is clipped to the even wedges of a fan from the
//   vanishing point and the other set, one row out of phase, to the odd
//   wedges. That is a true perspective checker with clip paths only. A band
//   that finishes its life restarts at the far edge exactly where the next
//   one is due (LIFE is even), so the loop has no seam.
//   Everything else sits on one LOOP-second timeline: the pointer clicks the
//   three tabs in turn, a dialog opens with a drag trail, the pointer answers
//   it, the picture in the second window fades from sunset to night and back.
//   At t=0 every window is complete, which is also the reduced-motion frame.

